# Packages-Live — the LIVE-ONLY layer of the Runink River ISO (profile.yaml
# `livefs:`, LiveOS/livefs.img). Never in rootfs.img; 20-clone-rootfs removes them from an
# installed machine (the install clones the running live system, which includes this layer).
# river lint profile-manifest checks that profile.yaml's livefs list is exactly this file.
#
# No artix-live-s6 / artix-live-base: the live session is configuration in live-overlay/
# (see the header of profile.yaml).

## The live GRUB scaffolding artools copies onto the ISO: the /usr/share/grub paths
## prepare_grub() reads out of THIS layer (cfg/*.cfg, locales/, tz/). OURS, built from
## build/pkgbuilds/runink-grub-live; it replaced Artix's artix-grub-live, whose MENU this
## image never used (iso-profiles/river/grub/ is the menu — see scripts/patch-artools.go).
## The PKGBUILD header lists, path by path, what artools reads and why each one is there.
runink-grub-live

## wpewebkit — the web view river-kiosk draws with, straight to the display through KMS/DRM
## with no X and no Wayland compositor.
##
## WHY IT IS HERE. river-live-fallback's chain is: a graphical session, else river-kiosk, else
## the text installer. On the workstation image the kiosk could NEVER run, because wpewebkit was
## in no package list at all -- so on a machine whose desktop does not start, the one GRAPHICAL
## path we advertise failed with "no web view (/usr/lib/wpe-webkit-2.0/MiniBrowser): wpewebkit
## is not installed" and the user was dropped to a text installer. That is what the owner hit on
## real hardware on 2026-10-03.
##
## It is LIVE-ONLY on purpose. An installed workstation has Plasma and a browser, so it has no
## use for the kiosk; 20-clone-rootfs removes this layer from the installed machine, which keeps
## the installed system's footprint unchanged. The cost is paid only on the medium that needs a
## graphical installer without a desktop.
wpewebkit
